@meta
id: 14-09
title: The whole word and nothing else
chapter: 14
type: lesson
skills: sub-basic
par: 28
minutes: 5
vscode: Ctrl-H with the "match whole word" toggle on
open: export.parser.ts

@brief
The variable `line` in this parser is being renamed `row`, because
`line` is what the product team call the items on an order and the
name has caused two bugs already. The `line` field on `ExportRow`
goes with it, and so does the comment that describes it.

Look at what else is in the file before you type anything:
`lineNumber`, `newline`, `pipeline`, `isOnline`. A pattern of `line`
matches inside every one of them, and `:%s/line/row/g` would leave you
with `rowNumber`, `newrow` and `piperow`. Chapter 13 gave you the
anchors that stop a pattern matching inside a longer word. This is
where they pay for themselves.

@keys
| `\<` `\>` | start of a word, end of a word — anchors, they match no text |
| `:%s/\<line\>/row/g` `Enter` | only `line` as a whole word |
| `\v<line>` | the same in very magic |

@hints
The problem is not the range and not the flags. It is that the pattern
is too loose. Tighten it.
--
`\<` matches at the start of a word and `\>` at the end. Wrap the
word in them and it can no longer match inside `lineNumber`.
--
`:%s/\<line\>/row/g` and Enter. Seven changes, and every decoy in the
file is left alone.

@solution
    :%s/\<line\>/row/g<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Twenty-three keystrokes, four of them anchors.

Word boundaries are the single most useful thing to add to a rename.
They cost four keystrokes and turn a dangerous substitute into a safe
one for the most common kind of collision, which is the short name
that appears inside longer names. `*` in chapter 12 added them for you
without asking; in a substitute you add them yourself.

They are not a complete answer. `\<status\>` still matches `status`
inside a string a customer reads, or in a URL, because a quote and a
slash are word boundaries too. The anchors know about letters, not
about meaning. The next lesson is about the cases the pattern cannot
catch, and the tool for those.

@start export.parser.ts
export type ExportRow = { id: string; status: string; total: number; line: number };

// The export is newline-delimited, one order per line, pipe-separated.
export function parseExport(raw: string) {
  const results: ExportRow[] = [];
  let lineNumber = 0;

  for (const line of raw.split(/\r?\n/)) {
    lineNumber += 1;

    if (line.trim() === '' || line.startsWith('#')) {
      continue;
    }

    const [id, status, total] = line.split('|');

    results.push({ id, status, total: Number(total), line: lineNumber });
  }

  return results;
}

export function isOnline(pipeline: string) {
  return pipeline.startsWith('online-');
}

@expected export.parser.ts
export type ExportRow = { id: string; status: string; total: number; row: number };

// The export is newline-delimited, one order per row, pipe-separated.
export function parseExport(raw: string) {
  const results: ExportRow[] = [];
  let lineNumber = 0;

  for (const row of raw.split(/\r?\n/)) {
    lineNumber += 1;

    if (row.trim() === '' || row.startsWith('#')) {
      continue;
    }

    const [id, status, total] = row.split('|');

    results.push({ id, status, total: Number(total), row: lineNumber });
  }

  return results;
}

export function isOnline(pipeline: string) {
  return pipeline.startsWith('online-');
}
